@defgroup micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-cpp-api-deprecated-literals Literals and Wrappers
@brief Deprecated literal operators and wrappers for typed operands.

@details
Literals such as `1_i` and `42_f` create strongly typed operands, whilst
wrappers like `mt::to_i{expression}` convert dynamic values, so that the
(deprecated) overloaded operators can compare and report them.

@par Deprecated
The literals of the `literals` namespace, the `to_*` type wrappers,
`to_t<T>`, and the two-argument `mt::approx (value, epsilon)` are
deprecated since 5.1.0, in favour of the function comparators `mt::eq()`,
`mt::ne()`, `mt::lt()`, `mt::le()`, `mt::gt()`, and `mt::ge()`, which
compare and report the plain values, and of the function comparator
`mt::approx (lhs, rhs, epsilon)`, which sets the tolerance explicitly. The
compiler reports their use.

The integer literals follow the C++ syntax: decimal, hexadecimal (`0x10_i`),
binary (`0b101_i`), and octal (`010_i`, which is 8), with digit separators
(`1'000_i`). A decimal point, an exponent, or a value that does not fit in the
type of the literal (e.g. `300_u8`) is a compilation error.

The floating-point literals are written with decimal digits and a decimal
point, optionally with digit separators (`1'000.000'5_d`), but without an
exponent; the digits before the decimal point, and the decimals, are each
limited to `std::numeric_limits<T>::digits10` of the type of the literal
(for example 6 for `_f` and 15 for `_d`), otherwise the compilation fails.

The literal `"name"_b` creates a named `bool` constant with the value
`true`, reported by its name; it is deprecated in favour of a plain `bool`
and a check message.

The floating-point literals (`1.5_d`) and wrappers (`mt::to_d{ 1.5 }`)
compare with a tolerance of one unit of the last decimal written (0.1 for
`1.5`), so that `1.45 == 1.5_d` is true. All six comparisons apply it
consistently: values within the tolerance are equal, and neither less nor
greater. Plain floating-point values compare exactly. The tolerance
depends on the digits written, and on which operand is wrapped; to set it
explicitly, use `mt::approx (lhs, rhs, epsilon)`.

@par Replacement

@code{.cpp}
namespace mt = micro_os_plus::micro_test_plus;

ts.test ("Check operands", [] (auto& t) {
  t.expect (mt::eq (answer, 42));
  t.expect (mt::approx (measured, 42.0f, 0.01f));
});
@endcode
